{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000caa5","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a088c6-4dd5-7de3-9b1f-066ae355783a"},"sequence":941,"recorded_at":1780531400001877,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"8693995b-bb36-49e2-b7e2-ad2e4fcb0749","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"8693995b-bb36-49e2-b7e2-ad2e4fcb0749","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"8693995b-bb36-49e2-b7e2-ad2e4fcb0749"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a088c9-08db-7ed0-bf2d-3656597b98d0"},"task_id":"01a088c9-08db-7ed0-bf2d-3656597b98d0","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a088c9-08db-7ed0-bf2d-3656597b98d0","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-37-1\",\n  \"command\": \"sed -n '1970,2000p' ~/.agents/skills/project-management/references/tracked-background-spawn.md 2>&1; echo \\\"===\\\"; sed -n '380,420p' ~/.agents/skills/project-management/references/tracked-background-spawn.md 2>&1\",\n  \"description\": \"Read handoff contract details\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"Provider `blocked` and a role that exits at board `blocked` use the same\\nStop-The-Line gate, including a role run without a bound goal. Board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped evidence\\npacket terminates the run with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; reviewer runs\\nalso persist the `stop_the_line` verdict. This branch never satisfies delivery\\nacceptance. Without the status/evidence pair, the boundary claim is recoverable\\nand the same revision is queued again.\\n\\nProducer provider exit is not handoff success. A `role_handoff` run must reach its immutable role end status and add or update a task-scoped outcome artifact relative to its launch snapshot. Zero exit while the board remains in `analysis`/`development`, or at the handoff status without evidence, queues autonomous same-revision recovery.\\n\\nAfter attaching the task-scoped outcome, producers should use\\n`task-board handoff TASK-ID --role ROLE`. The command resolves the configured\\nrole end status, verifies every checklist item and an actual stored task-scoped\\noutcome, then performs the evidence decision and ordinary status transition\\nunder one mutation lock. It reports all missing checklist/outcome evidence and\\nleaves status unchanged when the gate fails. Spawn-log artifacts do not count as\\nproducer outcomes, and the runtime still enforces its launch-relative outcome\\nsnapshot independently.\\n\\nA tracked producer's direct `set_status(..., status=to-review)` for its assigned\\nelement is refused with remediation to use this command. This is a spawned-role\\nworkflow guardrail, not a new authorization boundary for ordinary operators.\\n\\nDelivery success is not provider completion alone. Reviewer goals persist exactly one evidence-backed branch derived from board state: `accepted`, `changes_requested`, or `stop_the_line`; an exit while still `reviewing` triggers recovery. `required`/`light` leaves need board `done`, task-scoped producer outcome evidence, and a later reviewer whose persisted branch is `accepted`. `none` leaves need `done`, producer outcome evidence, and the owner’s completion-time evidence check.\\n\\n### Pattern E: Delivery Boundary (Watcher vs In-Session)\\n\\n- `spawn events`, `spawn watch`, and `spawn observe` are external watcher surfaces built on persisted run events.\\n- They can notify a human/operator and feed TUI/remote push adapters.\\n- They do **not** inject inbound text into the currently active orchestrator session by default.\\n- When the orchestrator session is launched through `task-board codex`, tracked terminal state is also injected back into the active Codex thread through the app-server runtime adapter.\\n- When the orchestrator session is launched through `task-board claude`, the manager owns the session PTY and types the terminal run state into it directly (`observation.delivery = claude-in-session`, `inSessionDelivery = true`). No background wait is required for the orchestrator to learn a run finished.\\n===\\nturns on role, task class, containing Story and local board alone, so an ordinary\\n`task-board spawn TASK-... --role developer --background` gets an isolated\\nworktree — and the half of the feature that makes that worktree reviewable\\nactivates on exactly the same conditions.\\n\\nIt runs **after** the spawn completion artifact guard, and on the same two facts:\\nthe board reached the run's end status, and the run attached a new or updated\\ntask-scoped outcome artifact. A run that attached nothing is reverted to its\\nproducer status by that guard and leaves no Change Request at all. A read that\\nFAILS is reported as a failure and never as \\\"did not hand off\\\".\\n\\nA workspace-claiming producer that publishes nothing says why, on the run's event\\nlog and in a board note: `handoff_unsatisfied`, with the detail — the board is\\nnot at the end status, or no task-scoped outcome artifact was attached.\\n\\nThe other two reasons are recorded nowhere, on one rule: a reason that is true of\\nevery run of its class says nothing about the run in front of you, and repeating\\nit on every completion buries the case the reporting exists for. An un-isolated\\nrun skips with `no_managed_workspace`, which is true of every un-isolated run. A\\nreviewer-archetype run skips with `not_a_producer`, which is true of every\\nreviewer run — a reviewer judges a candidate rather than publishing one, so\\nnoting it would append a line describing correct behaviour to the element's\\nprogress on every single review cycle.\\n\\n`repository_delta=empty` means the producer changed no repository file. It is an\\nattribute, never a state and never terminal: the Change Request is `ready` like\\nany other, a reviewer **is** spawned, and the injected prompt says in plain terms\\nthat nothing changed and lists the outcome resources that were produced. Deciding\\nwhether that was correct is a judgement about intent versus delivery, which is\\nwhat the reviewer archetype is for.\\n\\n**The kind is derived from the board, never declared.** A Change Request is\\n`task_delta` or `story_final`, and the second is what `worktree integrate`\\naccepts as the Story's integration unit. Publication asks the board whether\\ncompleting this leaf would also close its Story — the same question\\n`worktree checkpoint` asks to REFUSE that leaf, through the same call — so the\\nleaf one command declines is exactly the leaf the other treats as the Story's\\nunit. Every indeterminate answer refuses the publication: no board access, an\\nunreadable board, or an element the board does not contain means the question was\\nnever answered, and answering `task_delta` on a failed read would leave the Story\\npermanently unintegrable with nothing recording why.\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 5614,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 1404,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","final_result":true}}}
